<section id="abstract" class="content-section">
  <div class="section-container">
    <h2 class="section-title">Abstract</h2>

    <p class="lead">
      Foundation-model agents are being deployed faster than the community can characterize how they fail.
      In barely two years they have moved into browsing, software engineering, scientific discovery, and
      embodied control, where reliability is governed by <strong>long-horizon, closed-loop interaction</strong>
      that single-turn evaluation rarely captures. Failures already reaching users (error cascades, reward
      hacking, mode and reasoning collapse, memory drift, and over-reasoning loops) still lack a shared
      vocabulary, reproducible triggers, and comparable diagnostics.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      This tutorial gives a systematic, mechanism-first account of agentic failure modes across four axes:
      environments and infrastructure, training dynamics, evaluation and red teaming, and test-time scaling
      and adaptation, with safety threaded throughout. Attendees leave with
      <strong>reproducible triggers</strong>, <strong>comparable diagnostic protocols</strong>, and
      <strong>verifiable mitigations</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

